KNOWNBUG
main.c
-D_ENABLE_CHAIN_ --unwind 2
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
--
These spawn a chain of threads through a function pointer and assign the
resulting pointer to a shared variable. goto_symex_statet::assignment refuses
that outright:

    // see #305 on GitHub for a simple example and possible discussion
    if(is_shared && lhs.type().id() == ID_pointer && !allow_pointer_unsoundness)
      throw unsupported_operation_exceptiont(
        "pointer handling for concurrency is unsound");

so the run ends in EXIT=6 with no verdict. That refusal is the right
behaviour -- the alternative is a possibly wrong answer -- and
--refined-pointer-analysis does not cover this case (checked). Passing
--allow-pointer-unsoundness would make the test green by turning the refusal
into exactly the unsoundness it is guarding against, so it is not done here.

KNOWNBUG rather than deleted: skipped at CORE so the suite stays meaningful,
while test.pl -K still shows whether the limitation reproduces. The same
choice is made for the #305 false alarm in regression/datarace-por.
